1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,660
You bite a chili and your mouth is on fire.

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:04,453
But nothing is actually burning.

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:07,138
Chilies contain capsaicin.

4
00:00:07,138 --> 00:00:12,439
It latches onto TRPV1, a nerve sensor that normally detects painful heat.

5
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:15,480
1 — Your brain gets a false “HOT!”

6
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:15,893
alarm.

7
00:00:15,893 --> 00:00:18,921
Spiciness isn't a taste: it's a pain signal.

8
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:24,825
2 — Your body tries to cool down: sweating, a red face, a runny nose, watery eyes.

9
00:00:25,530 --> 00:00:31,333
3 — Scientists think the pain also triggers endorphins, which may be why some people love the burn.

10
00:00:32,030 --> 00:00:34,698
Water barely helps: capsaicin is oily.

11
00:00:34,698 --> 00:00:37,086
Milk or yogurt helps wash it away.

12
00:00:37,790 --> 00:00:43,465
After a chili challenge, get urgent help for chest pain, trouble breathing or nonstop vomiting.
